    "body": "## Summary\n\nImplements **ADR-021: Real-Page Interaction** — scrolling and incremental restyle. Wikipedia's HTML article (799KB, ~44,000 CSS px tall) exposed two gaps that unit testing had never caught: only the top viewport was visible (no scroll), and hover froze the UI for ~2-3 seconds (full relayout on every hover change).\n\nThree commits. Both gaps addressed. Hover latency improved 3-5x. **One honest undershoot: restyle is ~700ms on Wikipedia, not the \"instant\" the ADR aspired to.**\n\n## Commits\n\n| Commit | Scope |\n|---|---|\n| `473f00e` | ScrolledWindow wrap + `layout_height()` + `sync_drawing_area_size()` |\n| `728bc89` | `RenderPipeline::restyle()` + `restyle_walk()`; window wiring; CMake restructuring (pipeline + pango moved to `honeybee_lib`); dimension-preservation test |\n| `c364468` | Docs: ADR status -> Implemented, README row, CHANGELOG |\n\n## What was delivered\n\n### Scrolling (Commit 1) — fully met\n\nWrapped `m_drawing_area` in `Gtk::ScrolledWindow`. After every relayout, `sync_drawing_area_size()` sets the DrawingArea's size request to `(viewport_width, layout_height)`. GTK provides scrollbar, mouse wheel, trackpad, and Page Up/Down for free.\n\n**Visual verification** (Wikipedia HTML article):\n- Scrollbar visible on right\n- Article body reachable\n- References at bottom reachable\n- Small pages (link-test-a) fit viewport, no scrollbar (no regression)\n\n### Hover (Commit 2) — improved 3-5x, not yet \"instant\"\n\n`RenderPipeline::restyle()` re-cascades and updates paint colors in place, skipping layout entirely. Hover handlers (`on_mouse_motion` / `on_mouse_leave`) call `restyle()` instead of `relayout()`. The dimension invariant (restyle never changes `x/y/width/height`) is unit-tested.\n\n**Measured performance (honest):**\n\n| Page | Before restyle | With restyle | ADR aspired to |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| link-test-a (363B) | ~1ms | **~0.4ms** | instant ✓ |\n| Wikipedia HTML (799KB) | ~2-3s | **~700ms** | instant ✗ |\n\nBreakdown on Wikipedia: extract CSS 2ms, parse stylesheet 4ms, **cascade 690ms**, restyle_walk 5ms. The cascade dominates — 10,000 elements × ~17 UA rules + Wikipedia's inline style rules, recomputed on every hover transition.\n\n**What this means for the user:** hover on Wikipedia no longer freezes the UI for 2-3 seconds. It responds within ~700ms — noticeable but not the multi-second stall. The ADR's aspirational \"instant\" (sub-frame) requires the targeted cascade followup below.\n\n## Diagnostic corrections to the ADR plan (worth knowing)\n\nThe ADR's Implementation plan described three things. Reading the code before implementing revealed:\n\n1. **`LayoutText::element` backfill not needed.** ADR-018 backfilled `LineFragment::element`, and `paint.cpp` reads `LineFragment::color` (not `LayoutText::color`). So the restyle walk goes straight from `fragment.element` to computed style — no `LayoutText::element` field required. Simpler than planned.\n\n2. **The scrollbar bug was overlay-scrollbar event routing.** Default GTK4 overlay scrollbars wrap the child in a `Gtk::Overlay` that misroutes pointer events to children owning gesture controllers. Our DrawingArea owns `EventControllerMotion` (ADR-018) and `GestureClick` (ADR-019), so scroll events never reached ScrolledWindow. `set_overlay_scrolling(false)` was the fix — one line.\n\n3. **CMake restructuring was necessary.** `render_pipeline.cpp` and `pango_text.cpp` were in the `honeybee` executable, so unit tests (linking `honeybee_lib`) couldn't link against `RenderPipeline`. Moved both to `honeybee_lib` — they're GTK-free and belong there per the library's own \"pure testable code, no GTK\" comment. Added `PANGOCAIRO` pkg-config at top-level.\n\n## Followup: ADR-022 (targeted cascade)\n\nThe measured 700ms bottleneck is entirely in cascade. Only 2 elements' hover state changes per transition, but we re-cascade all ~10,000.\n\n**ADR-022 (proposed)** will add `cascade_one(element, ...)` + cache the previous restyle's `ComputedStyleMap`. On hover, re-cascade only the 2 changed elements and propagate inheritance to their subtree. Expected <5ms regardless of page size.\n\nOut of scope for ADR-021 because: new cascade API surface (ADR-010 territory), cache invalidation is subtle (what invalidates a cached ComputedStyle? how does inheritance propagate?), needs its own test suite.\n\n## Test count\n\n- Before: 253 cases, 986 assertions\n- After: **254 cases, 989 assertions** (+1 restyle dimension-preservation test, +3 assertions)\n- Zero warnings, zero failures\n\n## Verification checklist\n\n| Check | Result |\n|---|---|\n| Wikipedia scrolls top to bottom | ✅ |\n| Article body reachable | ✅ |\n| References reachable | ✅ |\n| Hover changes color | ✅ |\n| Un-hover reverts color | ✅ |\n| Hover responds within ~1s on Wikipedia (vs 2-3s before) | ✅ |\n| Hover <5ms on small pages | ✅ |\n| Small pages no scrollbar (no regression) | ✅ |\n| Restyle never changes dimensions | ✅ (unit-tested) |\n| Hover does **not** freeze for seconds | ✅ (was the original bug) |\n\n## Not included (deferred)\n\n- **ADR-022** (targeted cascade): the ~700ms → <5ms improvement\n- Scroll position restoration on Back/Forward\n- Horizontal scrolling\n- Scroll anchoring on resize\n- Partial repaint (only changed regions)\n- Hover affecting size (restyle would produce stale layout; UA doesn't do this, author CSS via `<style>` could; noted as v1 limitation)\n\n## ADR\n\n`docs/decisions/021-real-page-interaction.md` — status now **Implemented**, with an explicit Followup section proposing ADR-022.\n",
